  "verdict": "Measured: Zn-HAp (one Zn-substituted cell) on a (TiO2)16 cluster has 116 atoms and 1844 basis functions at 21e's tier (3468 at def2-TZVP); PEDOT:PSS as the doped polaron on it has 154 atoms, 1099 electrons (a doublet) and 1932 functions — 2.04× and 2.14× the pipeline's largest routine SCF at that tier (so2cf3, 904 functions, 1022 s), and about the size def2-TZVP already ran (so2cf3, 1989 functions). Estimated, low end optimistic and high end pessimistic: (A) B3LYP/6-31G(d) without dispersion — one SCF of Zn-HAp/TiO2 1.1–2.9 h, a relaxation 1.6–31.7 days; PEDOT:PSS 1.6–19.6 h and 2–213 days. (B) dispersion-inclusive at def2-TZVP (VV10 is native; its own grid cost is not included) — Zn-HAp/TiO2 8–17 h per SCF and 11–180 days per relaxation, PEDOT:PSS 13–240 h and 18–2620 days. Each is ONE configuration; adsorption sites, cluster sizes and terminations multiply it, and an adsorption energy relaxes the complex and both fragments. A certified minimum adds a Hessian ≈ 70 SCFs for RKS, and PySCF 2.11.0 has no analytic UKS Hessian for a VV10 functional, so the PEDOT polaron's minimum cannot be certified that way. So single SCFs and a B3LYP screen of one configuration (days to a month) are within reach of our CPU; a dispersion-inclusive, size-converged cluster study is months of one CPU for Zn-HAp and up to years for the PEDOT polaron at the pessimistic end — the scale at which a collaborator or a compute allocation matters; the periodic route (pyscf.pbc is installed) is not assessed here."
}
